---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';
import { site, url } from '../lib/site';
---
<Base title="Troubleshooting" active="troubleshooting">
  <header class="page-heading"><h1>Let’s find the snag.</h1><p>Setup errors happen. Start with the message you see, change one thing, then try the same command again.</p></header>
  <div class="inline-notice">Before changing anything: save your file, check the terminal name, and run <code>Get-Location</code> in PowerShell to check your current folder.</div>
  <section class="trouble-list" aria-label="Common setup problems">
    <details id="compiler-not-found"><summary>“gcc” or “g++” is not recognized</summary><div class="prose"><p>Windows cannot find the command. Confirm that the UCRT64 toolchain installation finished in MSYS2 and that your user PATH contains the correct <code>ucrt64\bin</code> folder.</p><ol><li>Check that <code>C:\msys64\ucrt64\bin\gcc.exe</code> exists in File Explorer. Your installation folder may differ.</li><li>Add the folder as its own user PATH entry; keep all existing entries.</li><li>Close every VS Code window and old terminal, then open a new PowerShell terminal.</li><li>Run <code>gcc --version</code> and <code>g++ --version</code> again.</li></ol><p><a href={url('guides/c-cpp/')}>Return to the compiler guide</a>. Do not download a random compiler ZIP to fix this error.</p></div></details>
    <details id="python-launcher"><summary>Python opens the Store, “py” is missing, or “py install” fails</summary><div class="prose"><p>The Windows app alias, an older Python launcher, and the current Python install manager can point to different programs. First identify what PowerShell can find:</p><pre><code>Get-Command py, python, pymanager -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue</code></pre><p>If <code>py install</code> reports that it cannot open a file named “install”, an older launcher may have priority. If the current manager provides <code>pymanager</code>, use <code>pymanager help</code> to confirm its supported commands before proceeding.</p><p>Follow the <a href="https://docs.python.org/3/using/windows.html#troubleshooting">official Python troubleshooting instructions</a>. Do not remove a working course installation or change app execution aliases without understanding which interpreter your subject needs.</p><p>After resolving discovery, check <code>py -3.14 --version</code> for this draft's proposed series.</p></div></details>
    <details id="file-not-found"><summary>“No such file” or “can't open file”</summary><div class="prose"><p>The terminal may be in a different folder, or the saved filename may not match the command.</p><ol><li>Run <code>Get-Location</code> in PowerShell, then <code>Get-ChildItem</code> to list that folder.</li><li>Check that the source file is listed with the exact extension: <code>.c</code>, <code>.cpp</code>, or <code>.py</code>.</li><li>In File Explorer, enable filename extensions. Fix a name such as <code>hello.py.txt</code>.</li><li>Open the correct folder in VS Code, save the file, and create a new terminal there.</li></ol><p>A path with spaces needs quotation marks when typed into a command. Keeping practice files together makes this easier.</p></div></details>
    <details id="program-wont-run"><summary>The compiler finished, but my program will not run</summary><div class="prose"><p>PowerShell needs <code>.\</code> before an executable in the current folder: use <code>.\hello-c.exe</code> or <code>.\hello-cpp.exe</code>.</p><p>Check the compiler output first. If there was an error, fix it and compile again before running anything. A leftover executable does not prove the new code compiled.</p><p>If Windows reports a missing runtime DLL, check that the same MSYS2 UCRT64 tools and their <code>bin</code> folder are being used. Do not download individual DLL files from unrelated websites. Report the exact filename and your tool versions to RDC.</p></div></details>
    <details id="wrong-terminal"><summary>“pacman” does not work, or a command looks unfamiliar</summary><div class="prose"><p><code>pacman</code> is an MSYS2 package manager command. Run it in <strong>MSYS2 UCRT64</strong>, opened from Start. The compile and run examples in the main verification guide belong in <strong>PowerShell</strong>.</p><p>Do not mix Windows commands with the brief Mac notes. A terminal is an environment, not just a box that accepts all commands.</p></div></details>
    <details id="git-identity"><summary>Git asks “Please tell me who you are”</summary><div class="prose"><p>Git needs a commit author name and email. Follow the <a href={url('guides/git-github/')}>identity step in the Git guide</a>, using your own values. Your commit email can be visible to others; use your actual GitHub noreply address if appropriate.</p><p>This is different from GitHub authentication. Setting a name and email does not sign you in or grant access to a repository.</p></div></details>
    <details id="git-authentication"><summary>GitHub push is denied or authentication fails</summary><div class="prose"><p>Confirm that the repository URL is yours or one you have write access to. For a club project, contributors usually push to their own fork and open a pull request.</p><p>Use the supported browser sign-in through a credential manager or GitHub CLI. Do not use your GitHub account password as an HTTPS Git password. See <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/get-started/git-basics/set-up-git">GitHub's setup and authentication guidance</a>.</p><p>Never put a token in a source file or paste one into a public issue. Do not force-push to a shared branch to bypass an error.</p></div></details>
    <details id="blocked-install"><summary>I cannot install software or approve administrator prompts</summary><div class="prose"><p>Stop and ask the device owner, school IT team, or instructor. Keep the exact prompt text. Do not disable security tools or try to bypass device restrictions.</p><p>You can read the materials and save source files while waiting. Ask whether an approved lab computer or browser environment can be used temporarily.</p></div></details>
    <details id="progress-missing"><summary>My setup checkmarks disappeared</summary><div class="prose"><p>Progress belongs to this browser and site address. It does not sync between devices. Private browsing, clearing site data, a different browser, or a change in hosting address may reset it.</p><p>The checkmarks are optional reminders. They do not record installation success or contribute to the pilot completion rate.</p></div></details>
  </section>
  <section class="prose" style="margin-top: 38px"><h2>Still stuck? Make the problem easy to reproduce.</h2><p>Include your operating system, terminal name, exact command, and first error message. Explain what you expected and what happened instead. Remove usernames, tokens, student IDs, and personal folder paths from screenshots.</p><p><a class="button" href={`${site.repository}/issues/new?template=content-problem.yml`}>Report a confusing step <Icon name="external" size={14} /></a></p><p class="muted">GitHub issues are public. This is a community feedback channel; an immediate reply is not guaranteed.</p></section>
</Base>
